deep.reconstruction:
Context (pp. 40–46). Grothendieck is building an axiomatic, purely order-theoretic 'analysis situs'. An 'atelier' (workshop) is a set 𝓕 of 'figures' with two relations, ≤ and ≪, satisfying axioms At 1–At 8. Inside it sit the 'lieux' L, point-like figures. The shadow ('ombre') of a figure X is omb(X) = {x ∈ L | x ≤ X}. The 'multiombre' of a figure is the family of shadows of its pieces. Page 44 ends a remark: an ordered set with a closed covering family, taking ≪ := ≤, gives a trivial example of an atelier. The top of this page closes that remark: among the examples to come, some are less elementary, and this one is the trivial example.

The new axiom, At 8 (the transcription's 'γ' is Grothendieck's handwritten 8). Let X ∈ 𝓜, the set of pieces. Its combinatorial boundary is ∂X = Sup_{≤} of the Y with Y ◁ X and Y ≠ X; here ◁ is 'is a piece/face of'. The axiom asks for a point x ∈ L with x ≤ X and x ≰ ∂X; Grothendieck glosses this as x̊ ≪ X, i.e. x lies in the interior. Set omb(X)° = omb(X) \ omb(∂X) = omb(X) \ ⋃_{Y◁X, Y≠X} omb(Y), the 'open cell'. Then (1.52) says omb(X)° ≠ ∅: every cell has a nonempty open part, so every cell is detected by a point.

Consequence. For a figure F, define multiomb(F) = {omb(X) | X ◁ F (i.e. X ∈ F̄), omb(X)° ≠ ∅}. This is a set-theoretic figure, a family of subsets of L. By At 8 the condition omb(X)° ≠ ∅ is automatic, so the index set is all of F̄. Grothendieck adds that these conditions are purely set-theoretic.

Corollary (finished on p. 46). For X, Y ∈ F̄ the following are equivalent:
- (a) X ≤ Y (⇔ X ≪ Y);
- (b) omb(X) ⊂ omb(Y);
- (c) omb(X)° ⊂ omb(Y);
- (d) omb(X)° ∩ omb(Y) ≠ ∅.

Proof sketch. (a)⇒(b)⇒(c)⇒(d) are immediate, using At 8 for (c)⇒(d). (d)⇒(a) needs the earlier axioms: a point of the interior of X lying under Y forces X ≤ Y. This is exactly the 'frontier condition' of stratification theory: an open stratum that meets a closed stratum is contained in it.

Role on p. 46. The corollary yields (1.53), a bijection Ssfig(F) ≅ Ssfig(multiomb(F)). An abstract figure is thus faithfully represented by a concrete figure made of subsets of the point set. It is a representation theorem, recovering a 'combinatorial stratified space' from its family of closed cells. It parallels the fact that a regular cell complex is determined by its face poset, and that points separate cells when open cells are nonempty.
